<h2>The idea</h2>

<p>
  Every visual decision in QDS lives in one place and travels one path. A raw value
  becomes a <strong>primitive</strong>, a primitive is given meaning as a
  <strong>semantic</strong>, and a semantic is applied by a
  <strong>component</strong> token. Nothing skips a tier, and nothing points
  backwards.
</p>

<p>
  That structure survives into the CSS rather than being flattened at build time,
  which is what makes a single stylesheet themeable:
</p>

<pre><code>--qds-component-button-primary-color-background-rest: var(--qds-semantic-color-background-primary-rest);
--qds-semantic-color-background-primary-rest:         var(--qds-color-blue-600);
--qds-color-blue-600:                                 #2563eb;</code></pre>

<p>
  Switching to dark mode redefines the middle line and nothing else. Try the theme
  toggle in the header — every colour on this page moves, and only
  {{ tokens.stats.modeVarying }} of {{ tokens.stats.total }} variables are
  redeclared to do it.
</p>

<h2>Components</h2>

{% example "Buttons, in every variant" %}
  <qds-button variant="primary">Primary</qds-button>
  <qds-button variant="secondary">Secondary</qds-button>
  <qds-button variant="ghost">Ghost</qds-button>
  <qds-button variant="danger">Danger</qds-button>
{% endexample %}

<p>
  Each of those resolves through the component tier, so
  <code>--qds-component-button-*</code> is at once the token API and the styling
  API. Overriding one of those variables is the supported way to restyle a button,
  and it is the same variable the token source defines.
</p>

